<a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="#id2777547">Licensing</a></dt><dd
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.1. <a href="#flic">What licensing
applies to the Ogg Vorbis
format?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.2. <a
href="#slic">What licensing applies to the included Ogg Vorbis
software?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.3. <a
href="#com">We make commercial, closed source software.  Can I
use Ogg Vorbis at all? What licensing do I need to pay?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.4. <a href="#sell">Are there
licensing fees for distributing, selling, or
streaming media in
the Ogg Vorbis format like there are in other formats, such as
MP3?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.5. <a
href="#money">If there aren't any licensing fees, how are you going
to make money
off the format?  Will you charge fees later, after Vorbis becomes
popular?</a></dt></dl></dd><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.  <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="#id2777730">Audio
Quality</a></dt><dd xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.1. <a href="#lossy">I've heard that
Vorbis is a &quot;lossy&quot; codec.  What does
this mean?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.2. <a
href="#sound">Does Ogg Vorbis sound better than MP3?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.3. <a href="#improve">Will Ogg Vorbis
audio quality improve?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.4.
<a href="#other">Why is Ogg Vorbis better than the other &quot;New MP3&quot;
codecs that are available?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.5. <a href="#transcode">Can I convert
my MP3 collection to the Ogg Vorbis format?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.6. <a href="#test">You're claiming
that Vorbis has great audio quality.
Have you done any listening tests to back this up?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.7. <a href="#bugs">I've heard some
test samples that had audible
artifacts.  Why did this happen?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.8. <a href="#quality">What does the
&quot;Quality&quot; setting mean?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.9. <a href="#speech">How does Vorbis
fare for speech compression?</a></dt></dl></dd><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.  <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="#id2778410">Features</a></dt><dd
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.1. <a href="#size">How big are Ogg
Vorbis files?  How do they compare to
MP3 files at
similar bitrates?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.2. <a
href="#bitrate">What is the maximum bitrate at which Vorbis can be
encoded?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.3. <a
href="#comments">Does Ogg Vorbis have the capability to show song
titles and artist information when the file is played or streamed?  I
don't see any in the test files.</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.4. <a href="#speed">How fast are the
encoders/decoders?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.5. <a
href="#video">Where's video?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.6. <a href="#stream">What about
streaming in Ogg Vorbis
format?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.7. <a
href="#support">What software and hardware support Ogg
Vorbis?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.8. <a
href="#container">Can I bundle Vorbis and another media type (like text
lyrics or pictures) in the same file?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.9. <a href="#unique">What other
unique features does Ogg Vorbis have?</a></dt></dl></dd><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.  <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="#id2778136">Development</a></dt><dd
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.1. <a href="#startdev">How do I get
started with Ogg Vorbis development?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.2. <a href="#bugfix">How do I
contribute a bug-fix or enhancement of the Xiph.org Ogg Vorbis
libraries and tools?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.3.
<a href="#fpsupport">Is it feasible to port the Vorbis decoder/encoder to a
platform without
floating point support (such as a StrongARM CPU)?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.4. <a href="#rc3-gcc2.95">Why doesn't
the latest release version compile properly on Linux-From-Scratch
systems?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.5. <a
href="#autogenrh7.3">Why can't I compile the latest CVS on Red Hat
7.3?</a></dt></dl></dd><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">6.  <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="#id2776070">Current
Affairs</a></dt><dd xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">6.1. <a href="#onepointoh">Version 1.0
is out! What does that mean?</a></dt></dl></dd><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">7.  <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="#id2776119">Meta-FAQ</a></dt><dd
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">7.1. <a href="#FAQmorequestions">I have
a question that isn't answered by this FAQ. Where can I turn for
help?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">7.2. <a
href="#FAQcontribute">Where can discussions about and contributions to this
FAQ be made?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">7.3. <a
href="#FAQcurrent">How current is this FAQ?</a></dt></dl></dd></dl><table
border="0"><col align="left" width="1%"/><tbody><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="qandadiv"><td align="left"
valign="top" colspan="2"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2825097"/><h3 class="title"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2825097"/>1.  What Is It?</h3></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="toc" colspan="2"><td
align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.1. <a href="#what">What is Ogg
Vorbis?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.2. <a
href="#names">What do all the names mean?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.3. <a href="#mean">Where do the names
come from?  What does the logo mean?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.4. <a href="#ext">What is the file
extension for Ogg Vorbis?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.5. <a href="#mimetype">What is the
MIME content type for an Ogg Vorbis stream?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.6. <a href="#replace">Does Vorbis
completely replace MP3, or is it just a
complementary codec?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.7.
<a href="#done">When will Ogg Vorbis be done?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.8. <a href="#artist">I'm an artist.
Why should I be
interested?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.9. <a
href="#fan">I'm a music fan.  Why should I be
interested?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.10. <a
href="#dev">I'm a developer.  Why should I be
interested?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">1.11. <a
href="#company">I run a music company.  Why should I be
interested?</a></dt></dl></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="what"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2825132"/><b>1.1. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What is
Ogg Vorbis?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Ogg Vorbis is a new
audio compression format. It is roughly comparable to other formats used to
store and play digital music, such as MP3, VQF, AAC, and other digital audio
formats. It is different from these other formats because it is completely
free, open, and unpatented.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="names"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2825160"/><b>1.2. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What do
all the names mean?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Vorbis is the name for
the specific audio compression scheme used
to
create Ogg Vorbis files.  It is part of the Ogg project, which is a blanket
project designed to create a fully open multimedia system.  Right now, Ogg
Vorbis is the only functional part of the Ogg project which has been
completed.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="mean"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2839198"/><b>1.3. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Where do
the names come from?  What does the logo mean?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org" target="_top">Xiph.org</a> has <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/xiphname.html" target="_top">a page</a> explaining
the sources and meanings of the names and logos.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="ext"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2825206"/><b>1.4. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What is
the file extension for Ogg Vorbis?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Since it is part of the Ogg project,
Vorbis files have the extension <i
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">.ogg</i>.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="mimetype"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777262"/><b>1.5. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What is
the MIME content type for an Ogg Vorbis stream?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><i
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">application/x-ogg</i></p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="replace"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777290"/><b>1.6. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Does
Vorbis completely replace MP3, or is it just a
complementary codec?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Ogg Vorbis has been
designed to completely replace all proprietary, patented audio formats.
That means that you can encode all your music or audio content in Vorbis and
never look back.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="done"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777318"/><b>1.7. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">When will
Ogg Vorbis be done?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">There are stable
reference implementations available now, and the file format has been
finished for some time.  A Vorbis file created today will still be
compatible with future decoders for years to come.  The format has been
designed to be flexible, so that the developers can continue to improve file
size and sound quality without &quot;breaking&quot; older encoders and
players.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="artist"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777349"/><b>1.8. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">I'm an
artist.  Why should I be
interested?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">There are a couple of
reasons:</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Although not all artists
realize it, MP3 is what is known as a &quot;lossy&quot; format.  Thus, much
of the sound data is removed when MP3 files are created.  This results in a
file with inferior sound quality to a CD.  Vorbis is also a
&quot;lossy&quot; format, but uses superior acoustic models to reduce the
damage.  Thus, music released in Vorbis will sound better than a comparably
sized MP3 file. </p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Also, artists
should be concerned about licensing terms for formats.  If you decide to
sell your music in MP3 format, you are responsible for paying Fraunhofer a
percentage of each sale because you are using their patents.  Vorbis is
patent and license-free, so you will never need to pay anyone in order to
sell, give away, or stream your own music.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="fan"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777440"/><b>1.9. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">I'm a
music fan.  Why should I be
interested?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">For one, Vorbis
provides a high-quality format for you to listen to your music.  For a given
file size, Vorbis sounds better than MP3, and is getting better as
development continues.  Vorbis already enjoys widespread player support and
should be compatible with several major hardware players soon.  With Vorbis,
you can listen to your music with higher quality in less space.  Also, using
Vorbis means your player and encoder choices aren't bound by licensing
terms.  Right now, you can only choose from a few encoders to create your
MP3 files, because most companies won't or can't pay the licensing terms for
encoders.  Using Vorbis lets you choose from a wide variety of
encoders.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="dev"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777478"/><b>1.10. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">I'm a
developer.  Why should I be
interested?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">If you develop hardware
or software audio players, you cannot distribute your work without being
affected by proprietary audio patent licensing terms.  With Vorbis, you can
create hardware or software products to encode or decode music files without
restrictions, royalty payments, or limits on distribution.  Vorbis also
provides a flexible, high-quality format that is of great interest to all
the audio geeks out there.  For more developer information, please refer to
our <a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/ogg/vorbis/index.html" target="_top">developer
site</a>.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="company"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777523"/><b>1.11. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">I run a
music company.  Why should I be
interested?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Music companies should
be very interested in the Ogg Vorbis format.
Other technologies require large financial investments to get started, but
Vorbis offers a unique platform that is easily reachable for growing
companies and a money saver for established businesses.  Because of its
wide player support and its open nature, your customers and clients will not
be plagued by incompatibilities and they will appreciate the higher sound
quality as well.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="qandadiv"><td align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777547"/><h3 class="title"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777547"/>2.
Licensing</h3></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="toc"
colspan="2"><td align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.1. <a href="#flic">What licensing
applies to the Ogg Vorbis
format?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.2. <a
href="#slic">What licensing applies to the included Ogg Vorbis
software?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.3. <a
href="#com">We make commercial, closed source software.  Can I
use Ogg Vorbis at all? What licensing do I need to pay?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.4. <a href="#sell">Are there
licensing fees for distributing, selling, or
streaming media in
the Ogg Vorbis format like there are in other formats, such as
MP3?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">2.5. <a
href="#money">If there aren't any licensing fees, how are you going
to make money
off the format?  Will you charge fees later, after Vorbis becomes
popular?</a></dt></dl></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="flic"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777565"/><b>2.1. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What
licensing applies to the Ogg Vorbis
format?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The Ogg Vorbis
specification is in the public domain.  It is completely free for commercial
or noncommercial use.  That means that commercial developers may
independently write Ogg Vorbis software which is compatible with the
specification for no charge and without restrictions of any kind.  However,
developers that wish to use the open source software we have written must
adhere to certain rules.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="slic"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777598"/><b>2.2. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What
licensing applies to the included Ogg Vorbis
software?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The bundled Ogg Vorbis
utility software is
released under the terms of the GNU GPL, or GNU General Public
License.  The details can be found at <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="http://www.gnu.org/"
target="_top">www.gnu.org</a>.</p><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The libraries and SDKs are released
under the more business-friendly
BSD license.  Please note that developers are still free to use the
specification to independently write closed-source implementations of
Ogg Vorbis which are not bound by these licenses.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="com"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777645"/><b>2.3. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">We make
commercial, closed source software.  Can I
use Ogg Vorbis at all? What licensing do I need to pay?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Again, there are no licensing fees for
ANY use of the Ogg Vorbis
specification.  As a commercial developer, you are free to create and
sell (or give away) open or closed source implementations of Vorbis
encoders, decoders, or other tools.  However, if you use our software
rather than writing an independent implementation, you must respect
the terms of the license.  Our libraries (available under the BSD
license) can be used whole or in part by closed source
applications.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="sell"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777680"/><b>2.4. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Are there
licensing fees for distributing, selling, or
streaming media in
the Ogg Vorbis format like there are in other formats, such as
MP3?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">No.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="money"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777704"/><b>2.5. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">If there
aren't any licensing fees, how are you going
to make money
off the format?  Will you charge fees later, after Vorbis becomes
popular?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The benefits of a
patent-free, license-free format outweigh the concerns of making money
directly from the format.  The Vorbis format will always be free and in the
public domain. Xiph.org is investigating a variety of models for
funding development, some of which may include licensing non-free
Vorbis-related programs and libraries to commercial projects.  One example of
this is Tremor, the fixed-point decoder for embedded systems.  Nevertheless,
the reference encoder and decoder will always be open source and third
parties will always
be free to modify or reimplement them.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="qandadiv"><td align="left"
valign="top" colspan="2"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2777730"/><h3 class="title"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2777730"/>3.  Audio Quality</h3></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="toc" colspan="2"><td
align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.1. <a href="#lossy">I've heard that
Vorbis is a &quot;lossy&quot; codec.  What does
this mean?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.2. <a
href="#sound">Does Ogg Vorbis sound better than MP3?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.3. <a href="#improve">Will Ogg Vorbis
audio quality improve?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.4.
<a href="#other">Why is Ogg Vorbis better than the other &quot;New MP3&quot;
codecs that are available?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.5. <a href="#transcode">Can I convert
my MP3 collection to the Ogg Vorbis format?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.6. <a href="#test">You're claiming
that Vorbis has great audio quality.
Have you done any listening tests to back this up?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.7. <a href="#bugs">I've heard some
test samples that had audible
artifacts.  Why did this happen?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.8. <a href="#quality">What does the
&quot;Quality&quot; setting mean?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">3.9. <a href="#speech">How does Vorbis
fare for speech compression?</a></dt></dl></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="lossy"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777748"/><b>3.1. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">I've heard
that Vorbis is a &quot;lossy&quot; codec.  What does
this mean?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
There are two broad classes of compression algorithms: lossless and
lossy.  Lossless compression algorithms produce compressed data that can be
decoded to output that is identical to the original.  Zip is a very common
example of a lossless compression format.  <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="http://flac.sourceforge.net/"
target="_top">FLAC</a> is a lossless compression
format that is specifically designed for audio.
</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
The other type of compression algorithm is called <i
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">lossy</i>.
This form of compression is very popular with multimedia data, like pictures,
movies, and sound.  Since these types of information are perceived by humans
with imperfect senses, the original data does not have to be reproduced
exactly.  Some of the information in the original file can actually be
discarded because we wouldn't notice it even if it was there.  Lossy codecs
can achieve much higher compression than lossless codecs by intelligently
discarding unneeded information.  In most cases, some loss of quality can be
tolerated, so even more data can be discarded, further increasing
compression.  MP3, RealAudio, and Vorbis all use lossy audio
compression.  This means that a Vorbis file, for example, will decode to a
WAV file that is different than the original.  The differences may or may not
be noticable, depending upon the quality selected during compression.
</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td
align="left" valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="sound"/><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777820"/><b>3.2.
</b></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Does Ogg Vorbis sound better than
MP3?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Yes, definitely.
Naturally, we invite you to judge this for yourself. Xiph.org's &#8220;<a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/ogg/vorbis/listen.html" target="_top">Dare To
Compare!</a>&#8221; page contains many samples you can use to make a quick
comparison, and we also encourage you to make comparisons from your own
music collection.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="improve"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777868"/><b>3.3. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Will Ogg
Vorbis audio quality improve?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Yes.  Vorbis has a flexible format
which allows significant tuning
of sound quality and training of the algorithms even after the file
format is frozen.  Vorbis sounds very good today, and will continue to
sound better every day.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="other"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777897"/><b>3.4. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Why is Ogg
Vorbis better than the other &quot;New MP3&quot;
codecs that are available?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Vorbis sounds better.  Vorbis is open,
so you're free to use it on your favorite platform. Vorbis doesn't have
intellectual property restrictions to get in the way.  And Vorbis doesn't
just try to sound better, it tries to do things fundamentally better in all
the ways that it can.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="transcode"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777928"/><b>3.5. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Can I
convert my MP3 collection to the Ogg Vorbis format?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">You can convert any audio format to Ogg
Vorbis.  However, 
converting from one lossy format, like MP3, to another lossy format,  
like Vorbis, is generally a bad idea.  Both MP3 and Vorbis encoders
achieve high compression ratios by throwing away parts of the audio
waveform that you probably won't hear.  However, the MP3 and Vorbis
codecs are very different, so they each will throw away different
parts of the audio, although there certainly is some overlap.
Converting a MP3 to Vorbis involves decoding the MP3 file back to an
uncompressed format, like WAV, and recompressing it using the Ogg
Vorbis encoder.  The decoded MP3 will be missing the parts of the
original audio that the MP3 encoder chose to discard.  The Ogg Vorbis
encoder will then discard other audio components when it compresses
the data.  At best, the result will be an Ogg file that sounds the
same as your original MP3, but it is most likely that the resulting
file will sound worse than your original MP3.  In no case will you get
a file that sounds better than the original MP3.</p><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Since many music players can play both
MP3 <span xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="emphasis"><em
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">and</em></span> Ogg files,
there is no reason that you should have to switch all of your files to
one format or the other.  If you like Ogg Vorbis, then we would
encourage you to use it when you encode from original, lossless audio
sources (like CDs).  When encoding from originals, you will find that
you can make Ogg files that are both smaller and better quality than 
MP3.</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">(If you must absolutely must
convert from MP3 to Ogg, there are 
several conversion scripts available on <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://freshmeat.net/search/?q=convert%20ogg%20mp3"
target="_top">Freshmeat</a>.)</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="test"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778010"/><b>3.6. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">You're
claiming that Vorbis has great audio quality.
Have you done any listening tests to back this up?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Over the last few public beta and
prereleases, we've fixed many of the
outstanding bugs that affected audio quality.
We're hoping to conduct
double blind
listening tests with a variety of test samples to fairly compare Vorbis
with competing audio formats.  Based on informal listening tests by the
developers and other interested parties, we are confident that Vorbis will
fare well in these tests.  These tests will be used to further tune the
format as development continues.</p><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">One such test has been completed, which
pits Ogg Vorbis against MP3PRO, WMA8, and AAC, all with approximate bitrates
of 64kbps. The results can be found at <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://ff123.net/64test/results.html"
target="_top">http://ff123.net/64test/results.html</a>. Other objective test
results comparing other file formats, or comparing at different bitrates,
are welcome.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="bugs"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778069"/><b>3.7. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">I've heard
some test samples that had audible
artifacts.  Why did this happen?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">While the Vorbis file format is
standardized, the Vorbis encoder has
undergone several beta and prerelease testing cycles.
If the files you heard were
encoded using an earlier version encoder, they might contain serious
audio quality bugs that have already been fixed.  Try re-encoding from the
source audio using the latest encoder. If you still think you've got a bug
that produces unreasonable artifacts, please e-mail the vorbis-dev list with
the details.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="quality"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778104"/><b>3.8. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What does
the &quot;Quality&quot; setting mean?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Beginning with libvorbis 1.0rc3, audio
quality is no longer measured
in kilobits per second, but on an arbitrary scale of 0 to 10, called
&quot;quality.&quot; This change in terminology was brought about by a
tuning of
the variable-bitrate algorithm that produces better sound quality for a
given average bitrate, but which does not adhere as strictly to that
average as a target.</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">This new
scale of measurement is not tied to a quantifiable
characteristic of the stream, like bitrate, so it's a fairly subjective
metric, but provides a more stable basis of comparison to other codecs
and is relatively future-proof. As Segher Boessenkool &lt;<a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="mailto:segher@xiph.org"
target="_top">segher@xiph.org</a>&gt;
explained, &quot;if you upgrade to a new vorbis encoder, and you keep the
same quality setting, you will get smaller files which sound the same.
If you keep the same nominal bitrate, you get about the same size
files, which sound somewhat better.&quot; The former behavior is the aim of
the quality metric, so encoding to a target bitrate is now officially
deprecated for all uses except streaming over bandwidth-critical
connections.</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">For now, quality 0
is roughly equivalent to 64kbps average, 5 is
roughly 160kbps, and 10 gives about 400kbps.
Most people seeking very-near-CD-quality audio encode at a quality of 5
or, for lossless stereo coupling, 6. The default setting is quality
3, which at approximately 110kbps gives a smaller filesize and
significantly better fidelity than .mp3 compression at
128kbps.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="speech"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778364"/><b>3.9. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">How does
Vorbis fare for speech compression?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">It works well, but is generally not the
optimal solution. Vorbis is designed
for the compression of music and general purpose audio.  Special 
purpose codecs can achieve much greater compression of speech than
Vorbis.  Vorbis also tends to have a latency that is too high for 
telephony, a common use of speech codecs.  Read the <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.speech.cs.cmu.edu/comp.speech/FAQ3.html"
target="_top">Speech   
Coding and Compression FAQ</a> for more details.  Those looking for an
open-source, patent-free speech codec should take a look at <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="http://speex.sourceforge.net/"
target="_top">Speex</a>.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="qandadiv"><td align="left"
valign="top" colspan="2"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2778410"/><h3 class="title"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2778410"/>4.  Features</h3></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="toc" colspan="2"><td
align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.1. <a href="#size">How big are Ogg
Vorbis files?  How do they compare to
MP3 files at
similar bitrates?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.2. <a
href="#bitrate">What is the maximum bitrate at which Vorbis can be
encoded?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.3. <a
href="#comments">Does Ogg Vorbis have the capability to show song
titles and artist information when the file is played or streamed?  I
don't see any in the test files.</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.4. <a href="#speed">How fast are the
encoders/decoders?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.5. <a
href="#video">Where's video?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.6. <a href="#stream">What about
streaming in Ogg Vorbis
format?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.7. <a
href="#support">What software and hardware support Ogg
Vorbis?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.8. <a
href="#container">Can I bundle Vorbis and another media type (like text
lyrics or pictures) in the same file?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">4.9. <a href="#unique">What other
unique features does Ogg Vorbis have?</a></dt></dl></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="size"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778428"/><b>4.1. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">How big
are Ogg Vorbis files?  How do they compare to
MP3 files at
similar bitrates?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Two files encoded at
the same bitrate, will always be the same size, if they are both encoded
with CBR (Constant Bitrate).  The current Vorbis encoder only encodes files
in VBR (Variable Bitrate) which can produce smaller files with better
quality, since it doesn't have to waste data for audio that is easy to
encode.  Files produced by the Vorbis encoder at the default quality will be
similar in size to 110kbps MP3 files, but will sound
better.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="bitrate"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778462"/><b>4.2. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What is
the maximum bitrate at which Vorbis can be
encoded?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Theoretically, there
isn't one.  Vorbis is tuned for bitrates of 16kbps
to 128kbps <span xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="emphasis"><em
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">per channel</em></span>.  But there's
nothing in the spec that says you
can't encode a file at 512kbps or 8kbps.  The current encoder
supports the following bitrates: 64-500kbps stereo and 32-256kbps mono (at
44.1kHz sampling rate). Lower bitrates will be officially available in
future versions.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="comments"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778498"/><b>4.3. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Does Ogg
Vorbis have the capability to show song
titles and artist information when the file is played or streamed?  I
don't see any in the test files.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Yes, Vorbis includes a <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/ogg/vorbis/doc/v-comment.html"
target="_top">flexible, complete comment field</a> for song and
artist info, as well as other track data.  The official encoder, oggenc,
allows you to enter comment info at encode-time. Other 3rd-party encoding
tools also let you enter or edit track data.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="speed"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778537"/><b>4.4. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">How fast
are the encoders/decoders?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Right now the encoder is about as fast
as most commercial
audio encoders (and about twice as fast as beta 3),
but not nearly as fast as some others.  Since we are focusing on
feature-completeness first and runtime speed second,
this is to be expected.  As the vorbis tools mature they will become
faster.  The decoding is roughly the same complexity as MP3 decoding, and
once the Vorbis decoding tools are optimized, they should decode at
similar speeds.  Decoding speed has increased 3-4x over the first beta
already, after the first stage of optimization.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="video"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778573"/><b>4.5. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Where's
video?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">If you're interested in
our progress on a video codec, check
out Theora at <a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.theora.org/" target="_top">http://www.theora.org/</a>.
Theora's 1.0 release is scheduled for
Summer of 2003.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="stream"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778609"/><b>4.6. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What about
streaming in Ogg Vorbis
format?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Streaming is an
important component of Vorbis.  The format has been designed from the
ground-up to be easily streamable.  The designers of Vorbis are working
alongside the creators of Icecast streaming media software to make Icecast
Vorbis-compatible.  We are also working on player support for streaming Ogg
files.  Streaming Ogg files from the web is possible with such popular
player software as WinAmp, XMMS, and ogg123.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="support"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778642"/><b>4.7. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What
software and hardware support Ogg
Vorbis?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Ogg Vorbis encoding
and/or playback is now native in a wide variety of
popular software.  It's included in popular players such as WinAmp, Sonique,
FreeAmp for Windows, and Unsanity Echo for MacOS.  It's also supported in
popular audio applications such as CDex, Siren Jukebox, and
GoldWave. For a more complete
list, refer to <a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.vorbis.com/download.psp" target="_top">our software
page</a>.  Ogg Vorbis is not supported by any publicly-available hardware
yet, but portable playback is possible for Sharp Zaurus owners by purchasing
<a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://thekompany.com/embedded/tkcplayer/" target="_top">tkcPlayer</a>
software from TheKompany.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="container"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777766"/><b>4.8. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Can I
bundle Vorbis and another media type (like text lyrics or pictures) in the
same file?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Yes.  The Ogg container
format was designed to allow different
media types to be multiplexed together.  In the future, Ogg movies
will most likely consist of a Vorbis audio track and a video track
(using Tarkin, VP3 or some other video codec) inside of a single Ogg
file.  Some preliminary work has been done to put MNG and MIDI content
into Ogg files as well.  Experimental code is available in the
ogg-tools module in the <a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/cvs.html" target="_top">Xiph.org
CVS repository</a>. Programmers working on such extensions can discuss
issues and questions on the <a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/ogg/vmail.html" target="_top">vorbis-dev mailing
list</a>.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="unique"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778584"/><b>4.9. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">What other
unique features does Ogg Vorbis have?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Vorbis has a well defined comment
header that is easy to use and extensible and obviates the need for clunky
hacks like ID3 tags.  Vorbis has bitrate scaling&#8212;a feature that lets
you adjust the bitrate of a Vorbis file or stream without reencoding; just
chop the packets up in the sizes you want them.  Vorbis files can be sliced
and edited with sample granularity.  Vorbis has support for many channels,
not just 1 or 2.  Vorbis files can be logically chained
together.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="qandadiv"><td align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778136"/><h3 class="title"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778136"/>5.
Development</h3></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="toc" colspan="2"><td align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.1. <a href="#startdev">How do I get
started with Ogg Vorbis development?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.2. <a href="#bugfix">How do I
contribute a bug-fix or enhancement of the Xiph.org Ogg Vorbis
libraries and tools?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.3.
<a href="#fpsupport">Is it feasible to port the Vorbis decoder/encoder to a
platform without
floating point support (such as a StrongARM CPU)?</a></dt><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.4. <a href="#rc3-gcc2.95">Why doesn't
the latest release version compile properly on Linux-From-Scratch
systems?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">5.5. <a
href="#autogenrh7.3">Why can't I compile the latest CVS on Red Hat
7.3?</a></dt></dl></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="startdev"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778154"/><b>5.1. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">How do I
get started with Ogg Vorbis development?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">It is important to first become
familiar with current development efforts.   The best ways to do this
are:</p><div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="itemizedlist"><ul
type="disc"><li xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Download and compile the latest
development code from <a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/cvs.html" target="_top">the Xiph.org CVS
repository</a>. The 
important modules for Ogg Vorbis development are ogg, vorbis, ao, and
vorbis-tools.  If you are interested in working on putting other media types
into Ogg files, the ogg-tools module also has example code.</p></li><li
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Subscribe to the <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/ogg/vmail.html" target="_top">vorbis user
and vorbis developer mailing lists</a>.</p></li><li
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Talk to developers in IRC.  They
usually are hanging around in #vorbis on
irc.xiph.org.  They can tell you more about what projects would be
interesting at the current moment.</p></li></ul></div><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Once you have seen what others are
doing, you will have a better chance to find a project to work
on.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="bugfix"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2777555"/><b>5.2. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">How do I
contribute a bug-fix or enhancement of the Xiph.org Ogg Vorbis
libraries and tools?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">If you want to
contribute bug fixes or enhancements to vorbis-tools, the
preferred method is to generate a patch against the latest <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="http://www.xiph.org/cvs.html"
target="_top">CVS code</a>. You can do this by running
the command &quot;cvs diff -u &gt; mybugfix.patch&quot; from the source code
directory you
checked out from CVS.</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Once you
have a patch, you need to file a bug/enhancement report on the <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="http://bugs.xiph.org"
target="_top">Xiph.org Bugzilla server</a>. You can attach your
patch to the bug entry.  If you consider the enhancement to be of general
interest or want comments from the developer community, you are encouraged to
post a message to the <a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.xiph.org/ogg/vmail.html" target="_top">Vorbis
development mailing list</a> describing the patch and linking to the bug entry
with your patch.</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Please note that
patches contributed under license terms not compatible with
the existing package license (BSD for libogg and libvorbis, GPL for libao and
vorbis-tools) will generally not be accepted.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="fpsupport"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778294"/><b>5.3. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Is it
feasible to port the Vorbis decoder/encoder to a platform without
floating point support (such as a StrongARM CPU)?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">The reference implementations of the
Vorbis decoder and encoder
provided on the
<a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.vorbis.com/download.psp" target="_top">vorbis.com download
page</a> depend heavily on floating point hardware.  While this presents no
problem for workstation-class CPUs (like x86 and PowerPC), it means
that embedded CPUs without floating point support (as are often found
in portable devices) are at a severe disadvantage.  There are two
possible ways to work around this problem:</p><div
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="itemizedlist"><ul
type="disc"><li xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Emulate the floating point instructions
-- Kernel emulation of
  floating point instructions is too slow to use for vorbis.  There
    are userspace libraries which provide floating point emulation that
      might be fast enough for your hardware.  Examples are <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://www.radiks.net/~rhuebner/mathlib.html"
target="_top">MathLib</a> for PalmOS and the -msoft-float support in
GCC.</p></li><li xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Change the vorbis library to use
integer-only instructions -- This
  is the more efficient solution, but also requires more specialized
    knowledge to implement.  Several people seem to have attempted it or
      are working on it now, but no free integer-based Vorbis decoder
        exists at the moment.  Xiph.org has an integer-based decoder library
	  available for licensing.  Contact <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="mailto:emmett@xiph.org"
target="_top">Emmett Plant</a> for details.</p></li></ul></div></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="rc3-gcc2.95"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2778487"/><b>5.4. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Why
doesn't the latest release version compile properly on Linux-From-Scratch
systems?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">It's a bug in gcc-2.95.
One workaround is to compile with -march=i586 instead of the default
-march=i686; real fixes already exist in newer versions of gcc.</p><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">&quot;Linux-From-Scratch&quot; users
encounter this bug most frequently, since that distribution uses the lethal
combination of gcc-2.95 and -march=i686 by default, but it may also affect
other distributions like Gentoo or users who choose their own compilers or
optimizations.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="question"><td align="left" valign="top"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="autogenrh7.3"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2776053"/><b>5.5. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Why can't
I compile the latest CVS on Red Hat 7.3?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Red Hat 7.3 comes with two versions of
autoconf. 2.13 is the default; CVS will only compile with
2.53.</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="qandadiv"><td align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2776070"/><h3 class="title"><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2776070"/>6.  Current
Affairs</h3></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="toc"
colspan="2"><td align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">6.1. <a href="#onepointoh">Version 1.0
is out! What does that mean?</a></dt></dl></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="onepointoh"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2776087"/><b>6.1. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Version
1.0 is out! What does that mean?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">For several months, and through several
beta and prerelease versions, the reference Vorbis encoder and auxiliary
software have been undergoing extensive bug-fixing, audio quality tuning,
and listening tests in order to make it the best quality possible. We are
now ready to offer Ogg Vorbis to the public in a complete, open, and free
software suite as an alternative to other more restrictive or lower-quality
music formats.</p><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">You are encouraged
to download the software, encode your favorite music, and compare its
quality to other compression schemes, and developers are encouraged to use
the software and format in their own projects.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="qandadiv"><td align="left"
valign="top" colspan="2"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2776119"/><h3 class="title"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2776119"/>7.  Meta-FAQ</h3></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="toc" colspan="2"><td
align="left" valign="top" colspan="2"><dl
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><dt
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">7.1. <a href="#FAQmorequestions">I have
a question that isn't answered by this FAQ. Where can I turn for
help?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">7.2. <a
href="#FAQcontribute">Where can discussions about and contributions to this
FAQ be made?</a></dt><dt xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">7.3. <a
href="#FAQcurrent">How current is this FAQ?</a></dt></dl></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="FAQmorequestions"/><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
id="id2776136"/><b>7.1. </b></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">I have a question that isn't answered
by this FAQ. Where can I turn for help?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">There are archived mailing lists for
advocacy, user discussion, and development at <a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" href="http://www.xiph.org/archives/"
target="_top">Xiph.org's mailing list page</a>, as well as an IRC channel
#Vorbis on IRC server irc.xiph.org, port 6667.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="FAQcontribute"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2776173"/><b>7.2. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Where can
discussions about and contributions to this FAQ be made?</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="answer"><td align="left"
valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left" valign="top"><p
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
href="http://xiph.org/archives/" target="_top">Xiph.org's mailing list
page</a> also has subscription info and archives of the Vorbis-FAQ mailing
list. Contributions and discussion are welcome there.</p></td></tr><tr
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" class="question"><td align="left"
valign="top"><a xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="FAQcurrent"/><a
xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" id="id2776210"/><b>7.3. </b></td><td
align="left" valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">How
current is this FAQ?</p></td></tr><tr xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
class="answer"><td align="left" valign="top"><b/></td><td align="left"
valign="top"><p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">It was updated on
2002-08-09 0332GMT.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></body></html>



<%
self.write(box.finish())
%>

<psp:insert file="footer.html" static="0">



